      "Contains soy lecithin and some whey concentrate"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Costco whey to buy if you compete in a tested sport or simply want the strongest certification for modest extra cost.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "25 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 120",
      "Third-party tested": "Informed Sport",
      "Best for": "Tested athletes, clean label"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "levels-grass-fed-whey-protein-5lb",
    "name": "Levels Grass Fed Whey Protein",
    "brand": "Levels",
    "badge": "Best Grass-Fed",
    "category": "Grass-fed whey concentrate",
    "description": "Levels has appeared at Costco in large bags, though stock is less consistent than Kirkland or Gold Standard. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop gives 24 g of protein and 5.4 g of BCAAs with no added sugar, from grass-fed, hormone-free whey concentrate. Flavors are sweetened with monk fruit rather than sucralose, and buyers rate the taste highly. It suits shoppers who specifically want grass-fed dairy at a warehouse-club price.",
    "pros": [
      "Grass-fed whey with no added sugar, per the label",
      "Monk fruit sweetening, no artificial sweeteners in the flavored versions",
      "Large bag brings the cost per 25 g down to the mid tier"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No third-party certification we could find",
      "Concentrate, so more lactose than an isolate",
      "Not stocked in every warehouse, and stock comes and goes"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A good grass-fed option when your warehouse has it; not worth driving across town for.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "24 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 130",
      "Third-party tested": "No certification found",
      "Best for": "Grass-fed, no sucralose"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "dymatize-iso100-hydrolyzed",
