    "description": "Isopure turns up at Costco in large tubs with a limited flavor choice. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop gives about 25 g of whey isolate protein for roughly 100 to 110 calories with essentially no carbohydrate or sugar, which makes it the leanest powder you are likely to find in the warehouse. It is also the most expensive per serving of the Costco wheys at typical prices.",
    "pros": [
      "About 25 g of protein for roughly 100 calories, the best protein density here",
      "Essentially zero carbohydrate and sugar, per the label",
      "Isolate base suits most lactose-sensitive stomachs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Mid-to-premium cost per 25 g, the highest of the Costco wheys",
      "Sucralose and an added vitamin blend in flavored versions",
      "No NSF or Informed Sport listing we could confirm"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Costco pick for strict low-carb or tight calorie budgets, not for pure value.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 25 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 100-110",
      "Third-party tested": "No sport certification confirmed",
