      "Dairy-free and USDA Organic",
      "Widely reported as one of the easier plant shakes to drink",
      "The most consistently stocked plant protein at Costco"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only about 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving, so the real cost per 25 g is higher than it looks",
      "Erythritol can upset some stomachs",
      "Consumer Reports placed it in its occasional-use group for lead"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The only realistic plant option at most warehouses. Buy it for taste and dairy-free convenience, not protein value.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 21 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 150",
      "Third-party tested": "USDA Organic; no sport certification",
      "Best for": "Dairy-free shoppers"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Costco Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-costco-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein powder at Costco?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, Kirkland Signature Whey offers the lowest cost per gram of protein, with about 25 g of protein and 1 g of sugar per scoop per the label. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard costs about the same and promotes Informed Choice testing, and Ascent is the best certified pick with Informed Sport status."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Costco have its own protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Kirkland Signature Whey Protein launched in 2024 in a 5.4 lb bag of roughly 70 servings, initially in creamy chocolate. Per the label it provides about 25 g of protein from a whey isolate and concentrate blend. Costco does not name the manufacturer, and we could not confirm a sport certification."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Costco protein powder cheaper than Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, for the brands Costco stocks. Warehouse prices on large tubs of Gold Standard, Ascent or Kirkland are usually competitive with or below Amazon's, especially during instant savings. Amazon has far more choice, and Subscribe and Save narrows the gap, so compare cost per 25 g of protein on the day you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Kirkland protein powder third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We could not find Kirkland Signature Whey in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases at the time of writing. Costco runs its own quality control on Kirkland products, but if you are a drug-tested athlete, choose a certified powder such as Ascent and confirm the exact flavor in the certifier's database."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Costco sell plant-based protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually one or two options. Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein is the most consistently stocked, giving about 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving per the label. Other plant brands appear occasionally, especially on Costco.com. If you need more protein per serving, you may have to look outside Costco for a dense pea isolate."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why can I not find a protein powder at my Costco?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Costco inventory varies by warehouse and region and changes during the year. Some products are online only, some are seasonal, and some are regional tests. Check the Costco app for warehouse stock, ask a member services employee, or order from Costco.com, which carries a wider range than any single warehouse."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Costco protein powder good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Any of the Costco wheys can help by making a high-protein, calorie-controlled diet easier to stick to. Isopure Zero Carb has the most protein per calorie, followed by Dymatize ISO100. Protein powder does not burn fat by itself, and our weight loss protein guide explains how to use shakes in a calorie deficit."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder",
    tags: [
      "best costco protein powder",
      "costco protein powder",
      "best protein powder at costco",
      "kirkland protein powder",
      "kirkland signature whey protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Costco Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Seven powders Costco actually stocks, ranked by what matters most in a warehouse club: how much protein you get for your money, then testing and formula.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#quick-comparison">Comparison Table</a>
      <a href="#how-we-ranked">How We Ranked</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#what-costco-sells">What Costco Sells</a>
      <a href="#is-kirkland-good">Is Kirkland Any Good?</a>
      <a href="#protein-per-dollar">Protein per Dollar</a>
      <a href="#certification">Testing & Certification</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy-what">Which to Buy</a>
      <a href="#other-costco-protein">Other Costco Protein</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best Costco protein powder for most people is Kirkland Signature Whey: about 25 g of protein per scoop and the lowest cost per gram of any whey in the warehouse at typical member prices. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard costs barely more and adds a known manufacturer and a testing claim, and Ascent is the pick if you need a strict sport certification. Costco's range is small and varies by warehouse, so we also explain what it tends to carry and how to compare tubs yourself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Costco Protein Powders Compared at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the short version before the detail. The table lists the seven powders we ranked, with the label figures that matter and a price tier based on cost per 25 g of protein at typical Costco member prices when we checked. Budget means roughly the cheapest third of the market, mid means middle of the road, and premium means you are paying for something other than raw protein value. Exact prices move week to week at Costco, and they differ between the warehouse and Costco.com, so we use tiers rather than dollar figures that would be out of date by the time you read this.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Certification</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kirkland Signature Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120-130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1-2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Choice (by flavor)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ascent Whey Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g or less</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget to mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Sport</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Levels Grass Fed Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None found</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dymatize ISO100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110-120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Choice</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Isopure Zero Carb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100-110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid to premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Plant-Based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid (per 25 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">USDA Organic only</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            All nutrition figures come from the brands' labels at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Two things stand out. First, the three cheapest options per gram are all whey blends, which is why Kirkland, Gold Standard and Ascent sit at the top. Second, the gap between the cheapest and the most expensive Costco whey is much smaller than the gap you would see on a supermarket shelf, because Costco only sells large sizes and negotiates hard on all of them. Even the premium end of this list is usually cheaper per gram than the same brand in a small tub at a grocery store.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only read one line: buy Kirkland Signature Whey for the lowest cost, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard if you want a known manufacturer and a testing claim for almost the same money, and Ascent if a strict banned-substance certification matters to you. Everything else on the list solves a narrower problem, such as lactose, carbohydrate or dairy avoidance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Ranked Costco Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is a label-and-price analysis, not a taste test. We did not buy and drink every tub. We built the list from products Costco has stocked in US warehouses or on Costco.com, checked each brand's current nutrition label and certification claims, and ranked them on protein per dollar first, then on testing and formula. Where we mention taste or mixing, we are reporting what buyers commonly say, not our own impressions. Our full approach is set out on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per dollar means cost per 25 g of actual protein, not cost per tub or cost per scoop. Scoops differ in size and protein content, so a cheaper-looking tub can be the worse deal. The calculation is simple: multiply servings per container by grams of protein per serving to get total protein, divide the price by that total, and multiply by 25. That is the number that lets you compare a 5.4 lb bag of 25 g scoops with a 2.74 lb tub of 21 g servings on equal terms. We rounded the results into budget, mid and premium tiers because Costco prices shift with instant savings, member coupons and online-only deals.
          </p>

          <p>
            After cost, we looked at third-party certification. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport test for banned substances and are the standards that matter to drug-tested athletes. Informed Choice is a related program from the same organisation as Informed Sport, with lower sampling frequency. A powder without a certification is not automatically unsafe, but a certification is independent evidence that what is on the label is what is in the tub, and that is worth something when the manufacturer is not named.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
