        <div id="what-costco-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Protein Powder Does Costco Sell?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Costco's protein powder range is small compared with a supplement store or Amazon, and that is by design. A typical warehouse carries somewhere between three and six powders at a time, each in one or two flavors and always in large sizes. The core is usually Kirkland Signature Whey and Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, with a plant option from Orgain and a rotating name-brand whey such as Ascent, Levels, Isopure or Dymatize. Costco.com carries a wider selection than any single warehouse, including some flavors and brands that never reach the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inventory genuinely varies by warehouse and by region, and it changes through the year. A product can be a nationwide item one season and a regional test the next. Ascent's 2025 move is a good example: per the brand, its vanilla whey went into warehouses nationwide and replaced the chocolate in stores, while chocolate stayed available online. Ka'Chava, SEEQ and other newer brands have appeared at selected Costco locations too. So treat any Costco protein powder list, including this one, as a guide to what Costco tends to carry rather than a promise of what is on your local pallet this week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Costco.com versus the warehouse</h3>

          <p>
            Online and in-store prices are often different. Costco.com prices usually include shipping, so they tend to run a few dollars higher than the same item on the warehouse floor, although online-only deals sometimes reverse that. Some items are sold online only, some only in warehouses, and some in both with different flavors. If cost per gram is your deciding factor, check the warehouse price on the app before ordering online, and watch the monthly member savings book, which features protein powder regularly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-kirkland-good" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Kirkland Protein Powder Any Good?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, yes. On the label, Kirkland Signature Whey is a perfectly normal whey blend: about 25 g of protein, roughly 130 calories and about 1 g of sugar per scoop, from a mix of whey protein isolate and concentrate. That is within a few grams and a few calories of Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, which has been the reference whey for decades. The main reason to buy it is simple: it is usually the cheapest per gram of protein of any whey Costco sells.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest caveats are about transparency rather than quality. Costco does not name the company that makes Kirkland whey, and we could not find an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for it. House brands rarely carry sport certifications, so this is not unusual, but it means you are relying on Costco's own quality control rather than an independent tester. For a recreational lifter or someone adding protein to breakfast, that is a reasonable trade for the savings. For a drug-tested athlete, it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other limitation is choice. Kirkland launched in a single chocolate flavor, and a 5.4 lb bag of around 70 servings is a lot of shakes if you find the flavor too sweet or not sweet enough. Buyer feedback has been broadly positive on taste and mixing, with some people finding it a touch thin in water. Because Costco takes returns, the risk is lower than buying a giant bag elsewhere, but a sensible approach is to mix your first few scoops with milk and adjust from there.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to compare Kirkland against the wider market rather than just against Costco's shelf, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> rankings run the same cost-per-gram maths across budget brands sold online. Kirkland is competitive with the cheapest of those, and it beats most of them once you factor in that you are already paying for the membership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-per-dollar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Best Protein Powder at Costco for Protein per Dollar
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein per dollar is where Costco earns its reputation. Because the warehouse only sells big sizes, even mid-priced brands land at a better cost per gram than they would in a 2 lb tub at the supermarket. The ranking within Costco is fairly stable, though: Kirkland first, Gold Standard close behind, Ascent and Levels in the middle, and isolates such as ISO100 and Isopure at the top end. Orgain looks cheap per tub, but its 21 g dose per two scoops pushes its real cost per 25 g up into the mid tier.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not cost per tub.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Servings per container times grams of protein per serving gives total protein. Divide the price by that, then multiply by 25. A tub that is cheaper on the shelf can easily be the more expensive protein.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sales shift the order more than anything else. Costco runs instant savings on protein powder several times a year, and a discounted name-brand tub can briefly undercut Kirkland. When Gold Standard or Ascent is on instant savings, it is often the better buy for that month. The member savings book and the Costco app both list current deals, and the deal-tracking communities that follow Costco post them as soon as they appear.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a wider look at where to buy protein cheaply, our guide to <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">buying protein powder on Amazon</a> covers Subscribe and Save, seller checks and how Amazon prices compare. Costco usually wins on the brands it stocks, and Amazon wins on selection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certification" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Certification at Costco
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only some of the Costco powders carry independent testing badges, and they are not equivalent. Ascent is Informed Sport certified, which means every batch is tested for banned substances before release. Dymatize ISO100 and Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard carry or promote Informed Choice, a related program from the same organisation with less frequent sampling. Kirkland, Levels and Isopure do not carry a sport certification we could confirm, and Orgain carries USDA Organic, which governs farming practices and says nothing about contaminants.
          </p>

          <p>
            A badge on the tub applies to specific products and sometimes specific flavors, so the only reliable check is the certifier's own database. Search the product name and flavor on the Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport sites before you rely on it. This matters most for athletes in tested sports, where a positive test is your responsibility regardless of what the label said, and for anyone buying for a teenager who competes.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, certification is a useful tiebreaker rather than a requirement. A well-known brand with a documented quality system and a long history is a reasonable choice even without a badge, and our page on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> explains where the real risks lie. Plant proteins, for example, tend to carry more heavy metals than whey on average, which is one reason the Orgain tub ranks last here despite being the only plant option Costco reliably stocks.
          </p>

          <p>
            If certification is your top priority and you do not mind paying more, our main <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings include NSF and Informed Sport options that Costco does not carry, and our <a href="/protein-powder-brands/">protein powder brands</a> guide explains how house brands like Kirkland fit into the market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy-what" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Costco Protein Powder Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If you just want cheap, decent whey</h3>

          <p>
            Buy Kirkland Signature Whey. It has normal macros for a whey blend, the lowest cost per gram in the building and a return policy behind it. If your warehouse is out of stock, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard is the near-identical fallback, often only slightly more per gram and sometimes cheaper on sale.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If you compete in a tested sport</h3>

          <p>
            Buy Ascent, and confirm the flavor in the Informed Sport database. It is the only Costco whey with an every-batch banned-substance certification, and its cost per gram is still far below most certified powders sold elsewhere. Dymatize ISO100 with Informed Choice is the second choice if you need an isolate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If dairy bothers you</h3>

          <p>
            Try an isolate first: Dymatize ISO100 or Isopure. Isolates carry far less lactose than concentrate blends, and many people who bloat on regular whey are fine with them. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference. If you avoid dairy completely, Orgain is the plant option Costco stocks most often, but take a larger serving if you are using it to build muscle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If you are cutting or eating low carb</h3>

          <p>
            Isopure Zero Carb has the best protein per calorie and essentially no carbohydrate, so it is the obvious choice if your warehouse has it. Our full guide to the <a href="/best-low-carb-protein-powder/">best low carb protein powder</a> covers the wider market, including options with no sugar alcohols.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, remember that powder is a convenient way to fill a protein gap, not the foundation of a diet. Most people do well getting the majority of their protein from food and using one shake a day where it helps. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide walks through daily targets by goal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-costco-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Other Costco Protein Products Worth Knowing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Costco sells a lot of protein that is not powder, and some of it competes on cost. Ready-to-drink shakes such as Premier Protein and Fairlife Core Power are sold in large cases. Bought by the case, they can come surprisingly close to powder on cost per gram, and they need no shaker or cleanup. For most people they cost more per gram than a tub of Kirkland whey, but if convenience is the reason you skip your shake, a case of bottles may be the better buy. Our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink</a> guide ranks the main options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen peptides, such as Vital Proteins, are also a Costco staple. Collagen is popular in coffee and is easy to mix, but it is an incomplete protein, low in leucine and missing tryptophan, so it is not a substitute for whey or a good plant blend if your goal is muscle. We left it out of the ranking for that reason. If you want collagen for its own sake, our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">best collagen protein powder</a> guide covers it separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meal replacements and blended nutrition shakes, such as Ka'Chava in the warehouses that carry it, are a different product again: more calories, more fiber, added vitamins and much less protein per dollar. They suit people replacing a meal, not people adding protein to their day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2.74 lb plant tub and a 5.4 lb whey bag are not comparable on sticker price. Work out cost per 25 g of protein, using servings per container and grams per serving from the label, and the rankings often flip. Smaller doses per scoop quietly push up the real price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming your warehouse has what the website shows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Costco.com and individual warehouses carry different products, flavors and prices. A product listed online may never reach your local shelf, and an in-store deal may not appear online. Check the app's warehouse inventory before you make a special trip for a specific tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a giant bag of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Costco only sells big sizes, so a flavor you dislike becomes months of grim shakes. If you have never tried a brand, read recent buyer feedback on that flavor, mix your first scoops with milk, and remember the return policy if it is genuinely undrinkable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a house brand as certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kirkland whey has reasonable macros and a low price, but we could not find a sport certification for it. If you are drug tested, buy a product that appears in the Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport database, and check the exact flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting collagen as your protein shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen peptides are cheap and easy to mix, but collagen is an incomplete protein with little leucine. It does not stimulate muscle building the way whey or a good plant blend does, so it should not replace your main protein powder.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

