            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder at Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Kirkland Signature Whey offers the lowest cost per gram of protein, with about 25 g of protein and 1 g of sugar per scoop per the label. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard costs about the same and promotes Informed Choice testing, and Ascent is the best certified pick with Informed Sport status.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Costco have its own protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Kirkland Signature Whey Protein launched in 2024 in a 5.4 lb bag of roughly 70 servings, initially in creamy chocolate. Per the label it provides about 25 g of protein from a whey isolate and concentrate blend. Costco does not name the manufacturer, and we could not confirm a sport certification.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Costco protein powder cheaper than Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Often, for the brands Costco stocks. Warehouse prices on large tubs of Gold Standard, Ascent or Kirkland are usually competitive with or below Amazon's, especially during instant savings. Amazon has far more choice, and Subscribe and Save narrows the gap, so compare cost per 25 g of protein on the day you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Kirkland protein powder third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not find Kirkland Signature Whey in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases at the time of writing. Costco runs its own quality control on Kirkland products, but if you are a drug-tested athlete, choose a certified powder such as Ascent and confirm the exact flavor in the certifier's database.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Costco sell plant-based protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually one or two options. Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein is the most consistently stocked, giving about 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving per the label. Other plant brands appear occasionally, especially on Costco.com. If you need more protein per serving, you may have to look outside Costco for a dense pea isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why can I not find a protein powder at my Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Costco inventory varies by warehouse and region and changes during the year. Some products are online only, some are seasonal, and some are regional tests. Check the Costco app for warehouse stock, ask a member services employee, or order from Costco.com, which carries a wider range than any single warehouse.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Costco protein powder good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Any of the Costco wheys can help by making a high-protein, calorie-controlled diet easier to stick to. Isopure Zero Carb has the most protein per calorie, followed by Dymatize ISO100. Protein powder does not burn fat by itself, and our weight loss protein guide explains how to use shakes in a calorie deficit.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
